---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import CtaBand from "../components/CtaBand.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What we can and cannot provide"
  description="The documents, samples and written claims we can provide before an order, and the certifications or factory audits we do not claim to hold at all."
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "Declarations" }]} />
  <section class="tight">
    <div class="wrap narrow prose">
      <h1>What we can and cannot provide</h1>
      <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        A supplier that cannot tell you what it does not have is difficult to verify. This page sets out the
        documents we can send before an order, the claims we do not make, and what to do if your compliance
        process needs something we cannot issue ourselves.
      </p>

      <h2>Documents we can provide</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Business licence copy</strong> — the registration record for {site.company.legalNameEn}, matching the USCC on the <a href="/about/">about page</a>. Sent on request before any payment.</li>
        <li><strong>D-U-N-S record</strong> — the D-U-N-S number is {site.company.duns}; it can be checked through the public lookup linked on the about page.</li>
        <li><strong>Material data sheets</strong> — face stock, adhesive and laminate data sheets from the material supplier for the grade quoted, where the supplier makes them available.</li>
        <li><strong>Digital proof</strong> — a PDF proof at final size before production, showing the artwork, bleed and cut line.</li>
        <li><strong>Physical sample</strong> — a sample on the actual container or material can be arranged before a full run; cost and timing are quoted with the job.</li>
        <li><strong>Supplier test reports</strong> — if a material supplier provides a report for a specific grade, we can request it and pass it on with the supplier’s name attached. We do not rewrite it as our own.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we do not claim</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>We do not own a printing factory.</strong> The work is specified, checked and quality-controlled by us, and produced by partner presses in China. The <a href="/about/">about page</a> explains the division of labour.</li>
        <li><strong>We do not hold an ISO 9001 certificate.</strong> We can describe our inspection process, but we will not display a certificate we do not have.</li>
        <li><strong>We do not claim FDA approval or food-contact certification.</strong> A label material may be intended for indirect food contact, but the documentation belongs to the material supplier and the compliance decision belongs to the brand and its regulatory adviser.</li>
        <li><strong>We do not claim a factory audit, a social-compliance audit or a sustainability certification.</strong> If your buyer requires one, tell us before the quotation and we will say whether the production partner can support it.</li>
        <li><strong>We do not publish customer names or logos</strong> without written permission, which is why there is no logo wall or invented case study.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>If your compliance process needs a certificate</h2>
      <p>
        Some buyers need a document that a trading company cannot issue: a factory audit, a product certification,
        a declaration of conformity for a material, or a test report in the seller’s name. We will not manufacture
        that document to win an order. Instead, we will:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Tell you what the material supplier can provide and in whose name it is issued.</li>
        <li>Ask the production partner whether the specific audit or test is available, and quote the cost and lead time if it is.</li>
        <li>Put the limitation in writing before you pay, so your compliance team can decide whether the job is viable.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If the answer is no, it is better to hear it before the deposit than to receive a PDF that does not survive
        verification.
      </p>

      <h2>How to verify us before paying</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Search the USCC <strong>{site.company.uscc}</strong> in China’s National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System (<a href="https://www.gsxt.gov.cn/index.html" rel="noopener">gsxt.gov.cn</a>).</li>
        <li>Check the D-U-N-S number <strong>{site.company.duns}</strong> through Dun &amp; Bradstreet (<a href="https://www.dnb.com/duns-number/lookup.html" rel="noopener">dnb.com</a>).</li>
        <li>Ask for a physical sample on your own container, or a short video call to see the labels being checked.</li>
        <li>Confirm that the invoice is issued in the name of {site.company.legalNameEn}; banking details should appear only on that invoice.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What this means for a first order</h2>
      <p>
        The honest position is narrow but clear: we can specify the material, check the artwork, place the job with
        a production partner, inspect the output against the approved proof and arrange shipment. We cannot turn a
        trading company into a factory, and we will not pretend that a certificate exists because a buyer asked for
        one. If that is the level of documentation your process needs, we would rather tell you now.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        Questions about a specific document: <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
        Company details are on the <a href="/about/">about page</a>; the editorial rules are on
        <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
